module lib/hf/accelerate
  What the SCF diagonalizes next. One SCF iteration maps a Fock matrix to a
  density and the density back to a Fock matrix; repeating that map as it
  is converges slowly or not at all when the density swings between two
  shapes. An accelerator chooses the matrix the next step diagonalizes from
  the recent ones: commutator DIIS (production), plain iteration, or
  damping, picked by `opts[OPT_ACCEL]`.

  DIIS (direct inversion in the iterative subspace, Pulay's method) keeps
  the recent Fock matrices and their errors, the commutators
  `F P - P F` in the orthonormal basis, which are zero at the solution. It
  finds the combination `F = sum c_i F_i` with `sum c_i = 1` whose combined
  error is smallest, a small linear system.

  The history is the record `Diis`: `hist` holds the matrices and their
  errors in a ring of fixed capacity, plus the matrix the last step used,
  `meta` holds the count stored, the newest slot, the capacity and the
  number of steps, and `gram` the inner products of the stored errors, one
  row per ring slot. It is filled in place and does not grow.

accel_capacity  @73e00ace
  why    A history of zero matrices would make every slot computation divide
         by zero, so the capacity is never below one.
  opts   the options column array; `opts[OPT_DIIS_N]` is the requested size

accel_meta_new  @f506e9b7
  why    The newest slot starts before the first one, so the first push lands
         in slot `0`.
  nd     the ring's capacity
  meta   the bookkeeping vector: count, newest slot, capacity, steps

accel_new  @c60753be
  why    The SCF makes one history per run, before the loop, sized for the
         number of densities and the orthonormal basis.
  k      how many matrices a step stores: one per density
  m      the size of the orthonormal basis
  opts   the options column array
  nd     the ring's capacity

accel_slot  @551ae360
  why    Every reader of the history asks for a step by how recent it is;
         this turns that age into a ring position.
  meta   the bookkeeping vector
  age    how many steps before the newest: `0` for the newest
  nd     the ring's capacity

accel_push  @c298152e
  why    Each SCF iteration adds its Fock matrix and error; when the ring is
         full the oldest step is overwritten
         [test "the ring keeps the newest nd steps and damping mixes with the last used matrix"].
  d      the history, written in place
  Fp     the newest Fock matrices in the orthonormal basis, `[k, m, m]`
  Ep     their errors, the commutators, `[k, m, m]`
  nd     the ring's capacity
  head   the slot the new step goes into
  s      a stored slot
  g      the new error's inner product with slot s's

  = g    the new error against each stored one: one row and column of gram, k inner products where a rebuilt DIIS matrix takes k (k + 1) / 2 [test "the inner products accel_push keeps are the ones recomputed from the history"]

accel_newest  @c8e4c15f
  why    Plain iteration, damping, and DIIS when its system cannot be solved
         all start from the newest matrix; this returns a copy (`slab`
         copies), so the caller cannot change the history.
  hist   the ring of matrices and errors
  meta   the bookkeeping vector

accel_solve  @6fb84851
  why    The DIIS system is small and can be nearly singular when two stored
         errors are almost parallel. Writing the elimination here lets it
         report that case instead of returning a meaningless answer, and
         keeps the arithmetic identical in both engines.
  A      the system's matrix, `[k, k]`, overwritten by the elimination
  y      the right-hand side, overwritten by the solution
  k      the system's size
  tol    a pivot below `tol` times the largest entry counts as zero
  big    the largest entry of A
  c      the column being eliminated
  p      the pivot row for column c
  r      a row below c
  t      a value being swapped
  f      the multiple of row c subtracted from row r
  i      the row being solved in back substitution
  s      row i's right-hand side minus the solved terms

  = big      the scale for the singularity test
  = p#2      partial pivoting: the row with the largest entry in column c
  = f        eliminate column c from the rows below
  = s        back substitution, from the last row up

accel_diis_coeffs  @d37a0c09
  why    This is DIIS itself: the error of a combination is
         `sum c_t e_t`, its squared size is `c^T B c` with
         `B_tu = <e_t, e_u>`, and minimizing that subject to `sum c_t = 1`
         with a Lagrange multiplier gives the bordered linear system below.
  G      the inner products of the stored errors, by ring slot
  meta   the bookkeeping vector
  k      how many of the newest steps to combine
  B      the bordered system: the error inner products by age, then a row and column of `-1`
  st     the ring slot of age t
  top    the largest diagonal entry of B, which scales the error block
  y      the right-hand side, then the coefficients and the multiplier
  ok     `1` when the system solved
  t      a step's age
  u      another step's age

  $$ \begin{pmatrix} B & -\mathbf{1} \\ -\mathbf{1}^\top & 0 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} c \\ \lambda \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ -1 \end{pmatrix} $$  [test "DIIS solves an affine problem in as many steps as it has directions"]
  = B#2      the inner products of the k newest errors, by age, from G (both spins summed for UHF)
  = top      scale the error block so the pivot test in accel_solve is relative
  = ok       solve for the coefficients and the multiplier

accel_cdiis_gram  @e9c7f1a4
  why    On an affine problem DIIS finds the solution once it has seen as
         many steps as the error has directions, which plain iteration does
         not [test "DIIS solves an affine problem in as many steps as it has directions"].
         Dropping the oldest steps when the system is singular keeps it
         working when the history holds nearly repeated errors, and with
         one step, or errors that are all zero, it falls back to the newest
         matrix [test "zero errors and a single step fall back to the newest matrix"].
  hist   the ring of matrices and errors
  meta   the bookkeeping vector
  G      the inner products of the stored errors, by ring slot
  sh     the shape of hist
  R      the combined Fock matrices, `[k, m, m]`
  k      how many steps are combined
  c      the DIIS coefficients
  ok     `1` once the system solved
  t      a step's age

accel_cdiis  @8d8eb609
  why    The registry member: a member called through `apply` gets no
         `gram`, so it recomputes the inner products from the history with
         the same inner product `accel_push` uses and gives the same bits as
         the production path [test "accel_extrapolate runs the member the registry holds at each option value"].
  hist   the ring of matrices and errors
  meta   the bookkeeping vector
  opts   not read

accel_inner  @09368ec1
  why    The one inner product the DIIS matrix is made of, used both when a
         step is pushed and when the matrix is recomputed, so the two agree to
         the bit.
  E      an error, `[k, m, m]`
  hist   the ring of matrices and errors
  slot   the slot of the other error

accel_gram  @78aeb37a
  why    The inner products of every stored error pair from the history
         alone, for a member called through the registry.
  hist   the ring of matrices and errors
  meta   the bookkeeping vector
  nd     the ring's capacity
  G      the inner products, `[nd, nd]` by ring slot
  s      a stored slot
  e      slot s's error
  t      another stored slot
  g      their inner product

accel_none  @69568ccd
  why    Plain iteration, the reference the tests compare DIIS with.
  hist   the ring
  meta   the bookkeeping vector
  opts   not read

accel_damp  @9e7ca2fe
  why    Mixing each new matrix with the previous one cannot speed up slow
         convergence, but it can turn an oscillation between two densities
         into convergence.
  hist   the ring, whose slot `2 nd` holds the matrix the previous step used
  meta   the bookkeeping vector
  opts   the options column array; `opts[OPT_DAMP]` is the weight of the old matrix
  R      the newest matrices
  a      the weight of the previous matrix, the default when unset

accel_methods  @c45c6336
  why    The SCF picks its accelerator by number, and tests compare them.

accel_extrapolate  @30e23b55
  why    The SCF calls one def whatever accelerator the options pick, and
         this def calls each member by name, so the default options reach
         CDIIS without a registry. The matrix it returns is stored, because
         damping needs the matrix the previous step used, not the previous
         step's raw Fock matrix.
  d      the history, written in place; its `gram` goes to CDIIS
  opts   the options column array; `opts[OPT_ACCEL]` picks the member
  k      the member the options name: `ACCEL_CDIIS`, `ACCEL_NONE` or `ACCEL_DAMP`
  R      the matrices for the next diagonalization

accel_t_opts  @96d3a465
  why    The tests pick members and history sizes by number.
  k      the accelerator member
  n      the history size

accel_t_system  @46326bac
  why    The linear test problem: a small symmetric matrix A. Its residual is
         affine in the unknowns, which is where DIIS's exactness can be
         checked.

accel_t_residual  @f97041c7
  why    The test problem's error: the residual of the linear system at x,
         with the unknowns stored as a stand-in Fock matrix.
  x      the unknowns, as a `[1, 2, 2]` stand-in Fock matrix
  e      the residual, written in place in the same shape
  A      the test system's matrix
  y      its right-hand side
  worst  the largest residual entry
  s      one residual entry

accel_t_run  @7db5f4c6
  why    Runs Richardson's iteration `x -> x + (y - A x)`, the fixed-point map
         of the test problem, under one accelerator, as the SCF would.
  k      the accelerator member
  steps  how many steps
  o      the options with accelerator k
  d      the history
  x      the unknowns
  e      the residual at x
  it     the step
